    "text": "Mock Research Document for PDF Extraction\nTesting\n\nOmkar Patil\u2018, Alex Chen?\nIndependent Researcher %Data Systems Lab\n\nAbstract \u2014 This mock document contains common formatting structures encountered in research\npapers and technical documents. It includes headings, lists, tables, pseudo twolcolumn layouts,\nfootnotes, code blocks, captions, and mixed text styles. The goal is to evaluate how well document\nparsers and OCR pipelines preserve logical reading order and structure for downstream RAG\npipelines.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nModern retrievallaugmented generation (RAG) systems depend heavily on reliable document\nparsing. However, PDFs often contain complex layouts such as multilicolumn text, tables,\nmathematical expressions, and captions. Incorrect ordering during extraction can lead to corrupted\nsemantic chunks.\n\n2. Background 3. Typical Parsing Pipeline\nPDFs are primarily designed for visual fidelity rather A common pipeline for RAG ingestion consists of the\nthan semantic structure. Libraries often rely on following stages:\n\npositional heuristics to infer reading order.\nChallenges include:\n\n1\n\n2\n3\n4\n\nMultilcolumn layouts\nFigures with captions\nTables with merged cells\n\n1 PDF text extraction\nLayout reconstruction\nSemantic chunking\nEmbedding generation\n\na b~ WD\n\nVector database indexing\n\nFootnotes and references\n\nTable 1: Example Structured Data\n\n[Modol __| paramotes [ Year [Nows |\nGPTE3 175B 2020 | Large language model\n\nBERT 340M 2018 | Bidirectional transformer\nResNetl50 2015 | Gomputer vision CNN\n\nFigure 1: Example diagram placeholder. In real documents, figures may appear between\nparagraphs and disrupt reading order during naive extraction.\n\nCode Example\n\ndef chunk_document (text, chunk_size=500):\nwords = text.split()",
